From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: pkgdemon <pkgdemonteam@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 14:33:39 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] NextBSD: Linuxulator: DRM wait timeouts return Linux ETIME,
 not ETIMEDOUT

Linux DRM drivers report a wait that timed out as -ETIME: v3d's
WAIT_BO, drm_syncobj's WAIT and TIMELINE_WAIT, and the other GEM/BO and
fence waits. Linux userland depends on it. Mesa and libdrm take ETIME to
mean "still busy" and treat anything else as an error; Mesa's v3d
v3d_bo_wait() aborts on it ("wait failed: -110").

FreeBSD has no ETIME, so LinuxKPI defines ETIME as ETIMEDOUT and
drm-kmod's -ETIME leaves sys_ioctl() as ETIMEDOUT. linux_ioctl_drm()
passed that through, and linux_errtbl turned it into Linux ETIMEDOUT
(110). Linux GL/EGL programs on the Raspberry Pi 5's v3d, and Linux
Chromium's GPU process, aborted the first time they polled a busy buffer.

linux_ioctl_drm() now returns Linux ETIME (62, LINUX_ENOTIME) when a
DRM ioctl fails with ETIMEDOUT. No FreeBSD errno maps to it in
linux_errtbl, so the handler puts the negative Linux errno in
td_retval[0] and returns 0; every Linux sysvec copies td_retval[0] into
the return register unchanged, which is how a Linux syscall reports an
error. Only ioctls in the Linux DRM range ('d', 0x6400-0x64ff) from
Linux processes go through here. Native callers keep getting ETIMEDOUT,
which native Mesa defines ETIME as.

Fixes nextbsd/nextbsd-kernel#424

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 sys/compat/linux/linux_ioctl.c | 20 +++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 19 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/sys/compat/linux/linux_ioctl.c b/sys/compat/linux/linux_ioctl.c
index f6c239b..7a08c53 100644
--- a/sys/compat/linux/linux_ioctl.c
+++ b/sys/compat/linux/linux_ioctl.c
@@ -69,6 +69,7 @@
 #endif
 
 #include <compat/linux/linux_common.h>
+#include <compat/linux/linux_errno.h>
 #include <compat/linux/linux_ioctl.h>
 #include <compat/linux/linux_mib.h>
 #include <compat/linux/linux_socket.h>
@@ -2554,12 +2555,29 @@ linux_ioctl_private(struct thread *td, struct linux_ioctl_args *args)
 
 /*
  * DRM ioctl handler (sys/dev/drm)
+ *
+ * Linux DRM drivers report a wait that timed out (GEM/BO waits, syncobj and
+ * fence waits) as -ETIME, and Linux userland relies on that: Mesa and libdrm
+ * take ETIME to mean "still busy" and treat anything else as an error (Mesa's
+ * v3d aborts).  FreeBSD has no ETIME, so LinuxKPI defines it as ETIMEDOUT and
+ * the drivers' -ETIME arrives here as ETIMEDOUT, which linux_errtbl would turn
+ * into Linux ETIMEDOUT (110).  Return Linux ETIME (62, LINUX_ENOTIME) instead.
+ * No FreeBSD errno maps to it, so put the negative Linux errno in the return
+ * value, which is how a Linux syscall reports an error.  Native callers do not
+ * come through here and keep seeing ETIMEDOUT, which native Mesa expects.
  */
 static int
 linux_ioctl_drm(struct thread *td, struct linux_ioctl_args *args)
 {
+	int error;
+
 	args->cmd = SETDIR(args->cmd);
-	return (sys_ioctl(td, (struct ioctl_args *)args));
+	error = sys_ioctl(td, (struct ioctl_args *)args);
+	if (error == ETIMEDOUT) {
+		td->td_retval[0] = -LINUX_ENOTIME;
+		error = 0;
+	}
+	return (error);
 }
 
 #ifdef COMPAT_LINUX32
